module display_generic.pos_3

// AN `Option[int]` IN A SLOT. THIS PROGRAM USED TO LIVE IN `neg_1`, where it
// asserted a refusal; it now asserts agreement with the oracle, byte for byte.
// It was committed through `git mv`, but git will NOT show it as a rename and
// the note says so rather than leaving the reader to wonder: both texts were
// rewritten past the similarity threshold, so the commit reads as one file
// added and one modified. The link between them is this sentence.
//
// WHAT LIFTED IT, measured 2026-09-19 at 01:09. Nothing in the compiler. The
// refusal was never about novac's ability to spell or emit this call -- the
// spelling landed the evening before (`c_instance_method` builds the oracle's
// family form `Nova_<Head>_method_<name>_<arg>...`, and `pos_2` beside this
// file prints an `Option[str]` with it). It was about the INTEROP SURFACE: an
// instance method reaches novac only through the symbols the shell carries,
// and the shell carries only the instances `novac/probe/shell_probe.nv` CALLS.
// The probe called `@display` on an `Option[str]` and on nothing else of that
// head, so `Nova_Option_method_display_nova_int` did not exist, and the slot
// judge refused BY NAME rather than emitting a call the linker could not
// resolve.
//
// The whole fix was one call in the probe plus a shell regen. After it, a grep
// of `shell.tpl.c` gives the symbol at :704 (declaration) and :6375
// (definition), and this file is byte-identical to the oracle.
//
// WHY THE LESSON OUTLIVES THE FIX: "this form is refused" and "this form cannot
// be compiled" are different sentences, and only the first one was ever true
// here. A refusal whose cause lives in a probe file expires the moment somebody
// edits the probe -- so the cause belongs in the fixture, in words, or the next
// reader spends a day in the compiler looking for a defect that is not there.

fn pick(f bool) -> Option[int] {
    if f {
        return Some(5)
    }
    None
}

fn main() {
    ro o = pick(true)
    println("${o}")
}
